  "conclusions": [
    "The timer-only B phase remained at 30 Hz with the Chrome 30 Hz override in all 27 display samples, so a 4 Hz JavaScript timer wakeup alone was not sufficient in that run.",
    "The 1 by 1 surface-update B phase was at 60 Hz without the Chrome override in all 28 and 27 display samples across two summary-valid runs. The control phases returned to 30 Hz in both runs.",
    "Together with the three 16 by 16 runs, the evidence attributes the observed transition prevention to page-surface mutation rather than the timer wakeup alone under these conditions."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "requestVideoFrameCallback missedCallbackFrames are callback-accounting evidence, not a direct physical scanout-drop count.",
    "The runs do not establish the Chromium or Android source-code owner.",
    "Black/white mutation of a 1 by 1 pixel is a diagnostic mechanism. Long-duration stability, power, thermal behavior, rendered pixels, perceptibility, and audible A/V sync remain unmeasured.",
    "The base runner returned validation code 10 with a display30-without-stable-raf-or-ui diagnostic classification while its errors array was empty. Variant validity comes from the timer-only or surface-damage summary and is kept separate from that base classification.",
    "The live input interval differs between runs, so small metric differences are not a performance effect size."
  ]
